WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.140
Most video games take themselves pretty seriously.

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:08.210
Sure, sometimes a game like Octodad or Sam
&amp; Max slips through the cracks, but by and

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:12.889
large, most games these days seem to be a
grim litany of post-apocalyptic despair.

00:00:12.889 --> 00:00:17.320
Which is why inside jokes are so important,
as they often provide a bit of much-needed

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:18.320
levity.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.630
The only problem with inside jokes?

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:21.420
Learning how to recognize them.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.869
Here's a look at some inside jokes only real
gamers will understand — and your guide

00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:29.790
to finally catching the gags you missed the
first time around.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:30.790
Zombie Genocider

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:35.179
You and your friends have probably competed
to see who call kill the most virtual zombies.

00:00:35.179 --> 00:00:39.440
But that probably pales in comparison to the
rivalry between the competing zombie titles

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.649
Dead Rising and Left 4 Dead.

00:00:41.649 --> 00:00:45.789
See, in Dead Rising, there's an achievement
called "Zombie Genocider" that players can

00:00:45.789 --> 00:00:52.899
earn by killing 53,594 zombies — the entire
population of the city the game is based in.

00:00:52.899 --> 00:00:57.309
Well, Left 4 Dead decided to literally one
up the by putting in their own achievement

00:00:57.309 --> 00:01:00.179
to kill 53,595 zombies.

00:01:00.179 --> 00:01:07.361
Naturally, Dead Rising 2 responded with a
new achievement requiring 53,596 kills, while

00:01:07.361 --> 00:01:11.850
Dead Rising 3 laughed at their own joke by
raising the total to 53,597.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:16.100
Well, it’s good to have goals, anyway.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.259
Whimsyshire

00:01:17.259 --> 00:01:21.759
When Blizzard announced Diablo 3, some fans
were furious because the game's color scheme

00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:26.289
looked too bright — which, sadly, is just
the kind of thing that gamers typically get

00:01:26.289 --> 00:01:27.299
worked up about.

00:01:27.299 --> 00:01:31.880
In response, Blizzard not only didn't change
the color scheme, they responded to the controversy

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:37.130
by adding a neon technicolored zone called
Whimsyshire, filled with rainbows, unicorns,

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.280
teddy bears, and happy clouds.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:44.220
Best of all, getting to Whimsyshire was extremely
complex and difficult, meaning all those super

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:49.090
hardcore fans who complained about the game's
color schemes had to work extra hard to find

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:50.090
it in order to complete the game.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:52.360
And you thought Vol'jin was Blizzard's best
troll.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:53.360
Dan Hibiki

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.460
When video game developer SNK released Art
of Fighting back in 1992, players couldn't

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.860
help but notice that the main character Ryo
seemed to be a direct ripoff of the popular

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.740
Street Fighter character Ryu.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.409
Capcom, maker of Street Fighter, noticed too.

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:11.890
They decided to get their revenge by adding
a secret character to their games called Dan

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:16.600
Hibiki, who was a parody of Ryo — and who
totally sucks in every possible way.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:18.840
"You look like you might put up a good fight!"

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.240
"Yeah, right… take your shot."

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:23.540
"Gahhh!

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:24.240
Gadouken!"

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:29.220
Ironically, while Art of Fighting and Ryo
have become almost completely forgotten by

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:34.489
the fighting game world, Dan Hibiki has survived
— and has even become a cult favorite among

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:35.760
hardcore gamers.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.490
Beating your opponent with the perpetually
overmatched Dan is considered a badge of honor.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:41.709
Daddy's back!

00:02:41.709 --> 00:02:46.760
Every now and then, Grand Theft Auto V's main
character, Michael, will randomly shout an...

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:48.440
unusual catchphrase.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:51.379
"Daddy's back, bitches!"

00:02:51.379 --> 00:02:56.349
It's in character for Michael to say something
like that — but it's also designed to drive

00:02:56.349 --> 00:02:58.549
a specific group of players crazy.

00:02:58.549 --> 00:03:02.629
That's because it's the first line from the
first mission in Grand Theft Auto IV, a game

00:03:02.629 --> 00:03:05.410
that's lived on for years in the modding community.

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:09.580
But most mods don't allow you to save the
game, so every time you play, you have to

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.890
start over from the beginning and hear this
line again and again.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:13.890
And again.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.150
"Again and again!

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:17.459
Again and again and again!"

00:03:17.459 --> 00:03:22.599
Making one of the lead characters of Grand
Theft Auto V randomly say it is just rubbing

00:03:22.599 --> 00:03:23.740
salt in the wound.

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:25.040
The Dopefish

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:30.319
The classic computer game Commander Keen describes
the Dopefish like this: "the second-dumbest

00:03:30.319 --> 00:03:34.790
creature in the universe, this creature's
thought patterns go 'swim swim hungry, swim

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:35.790
swim hungry.'"

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:39.999
Aside from his bulging eyes, buck-toothed
grin, and ability to fart on command —

00:03:39.999 --> 00:03:44.959
— there's nothing particularly special about
the Dopefish.

00:03:44.959 --> 00:03:49.730
Despite this, though, he's become a game designer
meme, appearing in Wacky Wheels, Quakes one,

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:55.060
two and three, Descent 3, Max Payne, Hitman
2, and many, many more.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.829
Just when you thought it was safe to go back
in the water…

00:03:57.829 --> 00:03:58.829
Gilgamesh

00:03:58.829 --> 00:04:03.530
From chocobos to Sid, the Final Fantasy series
has many recurring creatures and characters.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:08.659
But there's only one who has an in-story reason
for popping up again and again.

00:04:08.659 --> 00:04:13.439
While most Final Fantasy games don't connect
to each other directly, in Final Fantasy 5,

00:04:13.439 --> 00:04:17.720
Gilgamesh is trapped in a void outside of
space and time, leaving him free to show up

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:22.130
as a fourth wall breaking Easter egg in almost
every Final Fantasy game that's followed.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:30.600
Saints Row 4's voice options

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:34.480
One of the best things about the Saints Row
games is the series' detailed and flexible

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:38.790
character creation tool, which allows you
to make some truly bizarre protagonists.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.970
And that level of customization extends to
the voice options, to.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:46.630
You can give your Saints Row 4 protagonist
one of seven voices, including one called

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:47.980
simply "Nolan North."

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:52.810
"My fellow Americans, I know a lot of people
have concerns about the 'Sunday, Sunday, Sunday

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:53.810
Act!'

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:58.900
But I urge everyone to remember how f---ing
awesome monster trucks are."

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:03.450
Nolan North, of course, has provided the voices
for seemingly every character in video games,

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:07.630
ranging from Uncharted and Assassin's Creed,
all the way to The Last of Us and Destiny,

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:09.650
to name just a few.

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:13.640
Including him as his own, specific option
is pretty funny — and a great nod to one

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:17.490
of the most popular — and totally overexposed
— voices in gaming.

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:19.180
The Cucco Revenge Squad

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:22.800
It started as a joke in The Legend of Zelda:
A Link to the Past.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:27.000
But the Cucco Revenge Squad, which is a group
of angry chickens that descend from the skies

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:31.450
to kill you if you harass one of their feathered
brethren, has spread like a poultry plague

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:33.090
across the gaming landscape.

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:37.400
Beyond appearing in several Zelda games, the
Revenge Squad has also popped up in everything

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:41.830
from Super Smash Bros. to Guild Wars 2, and
even got a shout out in Hearthstone.

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:42.830
In short?

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:46.680
If you see a chicken in a video game, it's
probably best to leave it alone.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:47.680
Y'know, just in case.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:48.840
Thanks for watching!

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:51.670
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:54.060
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

